From: ACK72 <acikom72@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] airoha: test TX_FRAG_LEN 4 on the WAN MAC at 1 Gbps

TEST VALUE. This addresses PAUSE overshoot on one measured setup: a W1700K
(E2) WAN at 1 Gbps on a 100 Mbps line that an ISP enforces with PAUSE. It is
not a general fix. Keep it only until the conditions below are met or the
value is replaced.

With TX fragmentation off (TX_FRAG_LEN 31, set in post_config), the MAC
keeps starting frames for about 20 us after it receives XOFF. At 1 Gbps
that is two or three frames. An ISP that enforces a slower line with PAUSE
drops what exceeds its headroom: a single TCP upload stays near 27 Mbps
with 0.3% of its segments retransmitted, while a PC directly on the same
line reaches the line rate.

Measured during an upload, writing the register at run
time for 8 to 12 s per value:

  TX_FRAG_LEN  WAN TX    retransmitted  2+ frames after XOFF  FIFO full in pause
           31   27 Mbps  0.28%                           58%                 73%
           16  100 Mbps  0.009%                           7%                2.5%
            8  100 Mbps  0.002%                           9%                  0%
            4  102 Mbps  0.003%                           9%                  0%
            2   98 Mbps  0.012%                          11%                  0%

Writing 30 stalled TX within a second and the router needed a reboot.

What 4 means: TX_FRAG_LEN is bits 21:17 of the XSI MAC's GLB_CFG. The driver
and the vendor SDK use the maximum, 31, as "fragmentation off". The unit of
smaller values and how they change the reaction to PAUSE are not documented,
so 4 is not derived from a frame size or link rate. It was chosen because:
- With 4 the MAC finished the current frame and stopped on 91% of XOFFs under
  saturation, with the highest WAN TX of the values measured.
- 2 and 8 gave similar results, but 16 began to show a full FIFO during PAUSE
  and 30 stalled TX, so 4 stays away from the values that behaved worse.
- A copy of the vendor SDK, whose origin is not verified, sets 4 on its PCIe,
  USB and ETH MACs from E2 on. The same SDK keeps 31 before E2 and on this AE
  WAN MAC, the latter without saying why, so it does not vouch for this port.

Only the AN7581 WAN (PON PCS), E2 or later, at 1 Gbps or slower changes. Other
ports and faster WAN links keep 31. The value is set in pcs_link_up(), where
the speed is known and TX has been stopped since pcs_link_down() or
pcs_pre_config().

Required before applying this to every port and speed, or calling it a fix:
1. Meaning: find out from Airoha or from measurements what TX_FRAG_LEN controls,
   its unit and valid range, why 30 stalls TX, and why the SDK keeps 31 on the
   AE WAN MAC.
2. Long runs: hours of upload and download on this firmware with the TX FIFO
   underrun and overrun bits of INT_STS clear, no TX stall, and the value kept
   across reboots and link flaps. SMB reaches about 9 MB/s at PC links of 2.5G,
   1G and 100M.
3. Traffic: sustained 64 to 1514 byte frames, jumbo frames, PPPoE and VLAN,
   through hardware offload, the CPU path and hardware QoS.
4. Speeds: 10M to 10G on the WAN with a partner that sends PAUSE, and full line
   rate at each speed without underruns, including a real 1 Gbps uplink.
5. Ports: lan2 (GDM4, together with its FE fragment sizes), and the USB and PCIe
   SerDes.
6. Silicon: E1 stays excluded unless its flow control conflict is understood,
   and AN7583 and later revisions are measured separately.
7. Side effects: upload latency and simultaneous download compared with 31 and
   with a PC on the line, because PAUSE now limits the flow instead of drops,
   and no loss on other ports sharing the PSE buffers.
8. Link partners: no regression on lines that do not police with PAUSE, and on
   other ISPs that do.
9. Review: acceptance by netdev or Airoha, based on measurements and not on the
   unverified SDK copy.

diff --git a/target/linux/airoha/patches-6.18/9999-zzzzzzzz-0021-airoha-test-wan-tx-frag-len-4-at-1g.patch b/target/linux/airoha/patches-6.18/9999-zzzzzzzz-0021-airoha-test-wan-tx-frag-len-4-at-1g.patch
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/target/linux/airoha/patches-6.18/9999-zzzzzzzz-0021-airoha-test-wan-tx-frag-len-4-at-1g.patch
